ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS:
The DMS statement conversion tool cannot handle complex SQL Server statement blocks that include:
1. DECLARE statements with local variables
2. BEGIN TRANSACTION/COMMIT blocks
3. Multiple DML statements (INSERT, UPDATE) in a single batch
4. SCOPE_IDENTITY() function calls
5. Variable assignments (SET @Variable = ...)

These constructs are T-SQL specific and require procedural logic that the DMS tool
cannot convert as a single "statement" - they would need to be converted as a
stored procedure or function.

MANUAL CONVERSION APPLIED:
Converted the transaction block to PostgreSQL using DO $$ block with:
- DECLARE variables using PostgreSQL syntax (v_NewProductId instead of @NewProductId)
- INSERT ... RETURNING clause to replace SCOPE_IDENTITY()
- NOW() to replace GETDATE()
- Schema-qualified table names (productmanagement_dbo.products)
- Lowercase column names per PostgreSQL conventions

For the ADO.NET ExecuteScalarAsync pattern, provided simplified version:
INSERT INTO productmanagement_dbo.products (name, description, price, stockquantity)
VALUES (@Name, @Description, @Price, @StockQuantity)
RETURNING productid;

VERIFICATION STATUS: Manual conversion pending code integration and testing

